WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.720
October 22, 1962.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.060
US President John F Kennedy deploys a fleet
of warships to Cuba.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.070
To intercept Soviet cargo ships, which are
already on the way…

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.339
...transporting nuclear missiles to the island.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:16.770
Kennedy strategically called the impending
showdown:

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.560
KENNEDY: “a strict quarantine of all offensive
military equipment.”

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.270
What it really was, was a blockade – which
is an act of aggression.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:29.279
One wrong move on either side would trigger
an all out nuclear war.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:31.830
And it all started here, a week earlier.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.790
With an aerial photograph that doesn’t seem
to show much…

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:39.010
...unless you’re looking for something specific.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.890
Pretty much immediately following the allied
victory in World War II, the United States and

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.080
Soviet Union became bitter enemies…

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.220
...kicking off a decades-long struggle for
global influence known as the “Cold War”.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.140
Espionage and intelligence were at the center
of this conflict, most crucially surrounding

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.260
the mutual buildup of nuclear arsenals capable
of unprecedented levels of destruction.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:07.030
But the US initially had a hard time keeping
track of their nemesis.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:12.030
The Soviet Union was notoriously secretive,
and hid itself – and its actions – from

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.030
the world.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.600
CHURCHILL An iron curtain has descended across
the continent.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.100
Nobody knows what Soviet Russia intends to
do in the immediate future.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.570
Then-US President Dwight D Eisenhower saw
a solution that built on experimental intelligence

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.740
gathering from World War II:

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.740
Aerial photo analysis.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.060
In the late 1950s, the new high-altitude U-2
spy plane took photo reconnaissance to the

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.060
next level.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.670
It was equipped with a powerful camera and
could fly

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.130
at a staggering height of 70,000 feet…

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.460
...or, roughly 13 miles above Earth’s surface.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.500
“These cameras are described as capable
of spotting a golf ball on a putting-green

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.500
from 40,000 feet.”

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.280
In 1961, Eisenhower authorized the creation
of a new surveillance arm of the CIA: the

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.070
National Photographic Interpretation Center,
or NPIC.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.170
This small team of photo interpreters was
trained in photogrammetry...

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.140
...the science of determining measurements
from photographs.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.340
Using this method, an expert photo interpreter
could identify specific equipment hidden in

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.110
the tiny details of photographs…

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.720
...and recognize signs of nuclear missile site
construction.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.470
So what’s all this got to do with Cuba?

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.430
After the failed Bay of Pigs invasion in 1961,
where the US attempted to overthrow Cuba's

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.800
communist government, ties between the Soviet
Union and Cuba strengthened.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.900
The US worried that the Soviets might use
Cuba as a nuclear missile base.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.310
If so, they would suddenly have the Western
Hemisphere within range of nuclear weapons.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.630
The CIA began flying U-2 missions over Cuba
and bringing the imagery to the NPIC, whose

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.330
photo interpreters pored over every detail,
searching for evidence of Soviet presence

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:50.330
on the island.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.500
It was like looking for a needle in a mile-long
haystack – that’s how much film a single

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.220
U-2 mission yields, covering huge amounts
of land.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:05.130
But on October 15th, 1962, Dino Brugioni,
a senior photo interpreter, found something.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:09.420
This photo proved, beyond doubt, that the
Soviets were building nuclear missile sites

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.420
in Cuba.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.350
Brugioni identified military tents and trucks,
arranged in known-Soviet patterns.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:16.350
Launcher equipment.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:21.750
And, most critically, missile transport trailers
measuring 65 feet in length.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.379
Which, when compared to a photo taken in Moscow,
made it a perfect match for the Soviet SS-4…

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:32.870
...which had a range of 1,100 nautical miles,
meaning American cities as far as Washington,

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.070
DC would be in reach.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:39.440
When the NPIC briefed Kennedy on what they’d
found, the president ordered a scaling up

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.550
of U-2 missions to photograph and analyze
all of Cuba.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.070
Photo analysts updated Kennedy daily and in
secret on their progress, which gave him time

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.489
to decide how to confront the Soviet Union.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:54.269
Given the evidence, Kennedy was strongly advised
to launch air strikes against the missile

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:55.989
sites and invade Cuba.

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:58.019
But he took a more measured approach with
his –

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:01.580
KENNEDY: “strict quarantine of all offensive
military equipment.”

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:06.959
Which kicked off 6 intense days between the
US and the Soviet Union, with Soviet Premier

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:11.380
Nikita Krushchev calling Kennedy’s move
“an act of aggression that pushes mankind

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.129
to the abyss of world nuclear missile war.”

00:04:14.129 --> 00:04:18.579
ARCHIVE: Round the clock processing of their
film shows that work on the missile sites

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:19.709
is being accelerated.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:25.439
The damning photos were revealed to allies
at the United Nations, as the US military

00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:31.689
rapidly mobilized and was placed on high alert,
and Cuba prepared for another invasion.

00:04:31.689 --> 00:04:34.770
But when Soviet freighters reached the quarantine
line….

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:40.660
ARCHIVE: A Soviet-chartered vessel Amaruchla
is stopped, boarded, and inspected, then cleared

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.960
to proceed to Cuba.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.050
Apparently the Soviet vessels loaded with
offensive weapons have turned back.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.850
A few days later, Kennedy received a message
from Kruschev.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.999
The Soviet Union had agreed to withdraw from
Cuba in exchange for the US removing missiles

00:04:54.999 --> 00:04:57.289
it had placed in Turkey and Italy.

00:04:57.289 --> 00:05:01.409
So, the nuclear missile sites were dismantled
and the Soviets left.

00:05:01.409 --> 00:05:04.780
Transporting their nuclear missiles with them
back across the Iron Curtain.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:11.219
In a personal thank you letter to the NPIC,
Kennedy emphasized the importance of the “analysis

00:05:11.219 --> 00:05:15.389
and interpretation of the Cuban photography”
in advising the US’s response in what is

00:05:15.389 --> 00:05:17.360
now called the Cuban Missile Crisis.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:24.020
ARCHIVE: In summary: the Soviet Union did
embark upon a bold venture to establish clandestinely

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.770
in the Western Hemisphere a major offensive
weapons base.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.569
ARCHIVE: That they were deterred in this effort
is in large part attributable to the type

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:35.249
of reconnaissance photography that we have
just reviewed.

